• uncertain political and economic climates (including, for example, the United Kingdom’s 2016 referendum, commonly referred to as
“Brexit”, which has created economic and political uncertainty in the European Union); and
• reduced or varied protection for intellectual property rights in some countries.
These factors may cause our costs of doing business in these geographies to exceed our comparable domestic costs. Operating in international
markets also requires significant management attention and financial resources. Any negative impact from our international business efforts could
negatively impact our business, results of operations and financial condition as a whole.
We must keep up with rapid technological change to remain competitive in a rapidly evolving industry.
